// Package marks declares the marks that say what the compiler knows about a
// declaration: `builtin`, which names the IR construct a declaration
// dispatches to, and `intrinsic`, which names the native implementation that
// may replace a function's body.
//
// Unlike a user-facing mark such as `#[platforms.wildcard]` these annotate the library
// source itself: the checker reads them off a declaration as it registers it.
//
// The path is `sngl:internal/marks` and not `.../builtin`: `sngl:builtin`
// is the ambient package of built-in types, a different thing one letter away
// at the import line.

import ir "sngl:internal/ir"

// Names the IR construct a declaration dispatches to.
//
// The mark, rather than the declaration's name, is what the compiler keys on,
// so every built-in is shadowable by a user declaration of the same name. A
// type kind (`int`, `color`, `list`, …) marks a struct; a node kind (`window`,
// `timer`, `slot`, `errorBoundary`) marks a component. The kind says which
// construct, never which tier: the built-in visual nodes are declared in
// `sngl:ui`.
//
// A kind classifies one declaration and does not alias two — type identity is
// per-declaration — so a duplicated kind is an error rather than a silent
// overwrite.
func builtin(kind string) ir.Macro {}

// Names the native implementation that stands behind a function, and states
// what the compiler may assume about a call to it.
//
// The id is what a backend matches to emit that implementation; it is the
// declaration's own SNGL name (`string.length`, not StrLength). The flags
// carry the facts that have no other surface in SNGL — see
// `ir.IntrinsicFlag`. An id no backend answers to is a typo nothing else would
// catch, so the checker rejects one.
//
// A component may carry the mark too: it is then a primitive one platform's
// codegen emits from the declaration, and the id is that codegen's dispatch
// key, namespaced by the platform (`android:Column`) so the two id spaces
// never meet. It takes no flags — those describe a call, and a component has none —
// and it names no entry in the function registry, since the props and events
// are the declaration itself. The mark also keeps the inliner off the
// component: a platform's stdlib overrides lower down to its primitives, so
// the primitives must survive the pass that removes the overrides.
func intrinsic(id string, flags list<ir.IntrinsicFlag> = []) ir.Macro {}
